\section{A Legal Cover Depends on the Target}

Technology mapping replaces generic logic with primitives in a target library. It preserves the logic and sequential interface, subject to the behavior specified for unknowns, initialization, and black boxes. It must also map every state-holding construct to a cell with compatible clock, reset, enable, scan, and initialization semantics; a combinationally equivalent replacement with a different reset priority is not an equivalent sequential implementation. A mapped netlist says which cells are connected; it does not say where they fit or how long their wires will be. Some target passes do higher-level work first, such as recognizing adders for carry chains or matching RAM and multiplier blocks before the relevant word-level structure is lost.

In an FPGA, a mapper can cover a cone by a lookup table (LUT) if the cone has few enough distinct inputs. In an ASIC, coverage depends on whether a cell in the available library computes the needed function with particular pin polarities. A library can offer multiple drive strengths of one cell, each with different delay and area. Neither type of mapper generally tries every partition of the entire netlist. It enumerates manageable subsets, estimates arrival times and costs, and may recover area after identifying timing-critical regions.

The COMRADE flow described by Kasprzyk and colleagues calls the GLACE module generator; resource decisions begin well before all expressions become scalar gates. The asynchronous CASH flow of Venkataramani and colleagues emits structural Verilog and then performs technology synthesis and physical implementation. Thus even a lower-level-looking HDL file need not be target mapped. Examine the contents of the file and its cell dependencies rather than classifying it by its extension.

