\section{Encode One Function in a LUT and in Standard Cells}

Consider \(f=a\land(b\lor c)\). For a LUT indexed with \(a\) as the low address bit, \(i=a+2b+4c\). Exactly the indices \(3,5,7\) evaluate to \(1\), so the low eight configuration bits have value \(2^3+2^5+2^7=\texttt{0xA8}\). An iCE40 four-input LUT with its fourth pin tied low can be instantiated using Verilog-2005 technology-cell syntax:

\begin{verbatim}
module f_ice40(input a, b, c, output f);
    SB_LUT4 #(.LUT_INIT(16'h00A8)) lut (
        .I0(a), .I1(b), .I2(c), .I3(1'b0), .O(f)
    );
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

The upper byte is unused only because \texttt{I3} is tied low. This source depends on the iCE40 simulation model for functional simulation and on the device library for implementation. If writing generic source for multiple targets, use \verb|assign f = a & (b | c);| instead. An ASIC library with an OR-AND-invert cell can make \(\neg f\) in one cell and use an inverter for \(f\); if it lacks that cell the mapper must use a different cover. A neighboring negative-polarity consumer could use \(\neg f\) directly. \Cref{fig:visual-317} compares these choices without assuming equal delays or areas.

Pseudocode for choosing a combinational cover:

\begin{verbatim}
for root in topological_order(net):
    for cut in enumerate_bounded_cuts(root):
        table = evaluate_function(root, cut)
        for match in target_matches(table):
            score = delay_and_cost(match, arrivals_at(cut))
            record_candidate(root, match, score)
instantiate_cover_from_sinks()  # overlapping cuts need not all be used
check_netlist_equivalence()     # include primitive functional models
\end{verbatim}

