\section{Use the Mapped Inventory, Not an Abstract Gate Count}

With a generic module named \texttt{top} in \texttt{top.v}, the \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/techmap_synth.html}{Yosys technology-mapping guide} and its \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/cell_libs.html}{cell-library guide} motivate two distinct scripts. For iCE40:

\begin{verbatim}
read_verilog top.v
synth_ice40 -top top -json top_ice40.json
\end{verbatim}

For a simple ASIC design with a real target Liberty file \texttt{cells.lib}, start with a clean Yosys process:

\begin{verbatim}
read_verilog top.v
synth -top top -noabc
dfflibmap -liberty cells.lib
abc -liberty cells.lib
check
stat -liberty cells.lib
write_verilog -noattr top_stdcell.v
\end{verbatim}

The second script assumes the library covers the required sequential cells; real flows also handle memories, scan, tie cells, clocks, and physical-only cells. The \texttt{check} command catches obvious undriven or multiply driven connections, but it does not prove that no generic cell remains or that a particular library cell has the intended reset semantics. Check the cell inventory and unexpected generic cells before handing the netlist onward. Inspect LUTs, carry chains, hard blocks and register counts on FPGA, and separate cell area, buffer insertion and utilization on ASIC. Delay estimates at this stage use an incomplete physical model; the winning cover can change if placement puts loads far apart. Even exact Boolean equivalence does not guarantee fitting a package or meeting timing at a specified operating corner.
